WEBVTT

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:03.142
A practical quality workflow has five steps.

00:00:03.142 --> 00:00:08.522
First, profile the data. Second, detect potential issues.

00:00:08.522 --> 00:00:09.477
Third, investigate the source and context.

00:00:09.477 --> 00:00:16.342
Only then decide whether to correct, retain, exclude, impute, or flag the value.

00:00:16.342 --> 00:00:18.962
Finally, document what happened.

00:00:18.962 --> 00:00:27.332
This sequence creates an audit trail and reduces the risk of silently changing legitimate unusual values.

00:00:27.332 --> 00:00:31.042
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:31.042 --> 00:00:38.552
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:38.552 --> 00:00:40.412
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:47.562
In a real setting such as an e-commerce team preparing customer, purchase, and web-event data,

00:00:47.562 --> 00:00:54.692
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:54.692 --> 00:00:57.802
before discussing syntax.

00:00:57.802 --> 00:01:03.997
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
